---
title: "AI History — From Turing to Transformers"
---

# AI History — From Turing to Transformers
style: section

## Foundations of Computing (1936–1955) #era

  1936: Universal Turing Machine concept — "On Computable Numbers" #Turing #Milestone
  Establishes the theoretical basis for any computable process.
  Alan Turing defines a mathematical model of computation that can simulate any algorithm.

  1943: McCulloch-Pitts artificial neuron
  Warren McCulloch & Walter Pitts publish "A Logical Calculus of the Ideas Immanent in Nervous Activity".
  First mathematical model of a biological neuron — the foundation of neural networks.

## Birth of AI (1956–1969) #era

  1956-08: Dartmouth Summer Research Project — AI is born #McCarthy #Milestone
  John McCarthy organizes the conference where "Artificial Intelligence" becomes an official field.
  Attendees: McCarthy, Minsky, Shannon, Newell, Simon — the founding generation.

  1956: Logic Theorist (Newell, Simon & Shaw)
  First AI program to prove mathematical theorems — proved 38 of 52 theorems from Principia Mathematica.
  Newell & Simon claim: "We have invented a thinking machine."

  1966: ELIZA chatbot (Joseph Weizenbaum, MIT) #Milestone
  First conversational AI program. Simulates a Rogerian psychotherapist.
  Users form emotional attachments — raises early ethics questions.

  1969: "Perceptrons" book (Minsky & Papert) #Minsky
  Mathematically proves single-layer perceptrons cannot solve XOR.
  Triggers massive funding cuts to neural network research.

## First AI Winter (1970–1979) #era

  1970: Prolog logic programming language (Colmerauer)
  Declarative logic language becomes the backbone of symbolic AI in Europe.

  1974/1975: Back-propagation concept (Paul Werbos, PhD thesis)
  Werbos derives back-propagation for training multi-layer networks.
  Goes largely unnoticed for a decade.

  1974/1979: AI hype collapses — research labs defunded #FirstWinter
  Machine translation failures, chess programs plateauing, combinatorial explosion problems.
  Most AI promises from the 1950s–60s remain unfulfilled.

## Expert Systems Era (1980–1987) #era

  1980: XCON/R1 expert system deployed at DEC
  First commercially successful expert system — saves DEC $40M/year.
  Launches a billion-dollar expert systems industry.

  1982: Hopfield Networks (John Hopfield) #Milestone
  Energy-based associative memory network.
  Revives interest in neural networks; wins Hopfield the 2024 Nobel Prize in Physics.

  1984: Cyc project started (Doug Lenat)
  Attempt to encode all human common sense knowledge.
  One of the most ambitious (and controversial) symbolic AI projects.

  1986: Back-propagation popularized — Nature paper (Rumelhart, Hinton, Williams) #Hinton #Milestone
  "Learning representations by back-propagating errors" makes multi-layer networks trainable.
  Relaunches neural network research worldwide.

  1987: Lisp Machine market collapses — Second AI Winter begins
  Expert systems prove brittle, expensive to maintain, and unable to generalize.
  $500M+ AI industry collapses in two years.

## Second AI Winter (1987–1993) #era

  1988: Judea Pearl — "Probabilistic Reasoning in Intelligent Systems" #Milestone
  Introduces Bayesian networks for reasoning under uncertainty.
  Shifts focus from rules to probabilities — foundational to modern ML.

  1992: TD-Gammon (Gerald Tesauro, IBM)
  Reinforcement learning plays backgammon at human-champion level.
  First successful application of neural networks + RL.

  1993: DARPA cuts AI funding — winter deepens #SecondWinter
  After expert systems failures, US government refocuses on specific applications.

## Statistical AI Rise (1994–2005) #era

  1995: Support Vector Machines (Cortes & Vapnik) #Milestone
  Elegant margin-maximizing classifier with strong theoretical guarantees.
  Dominates ML benchmarks for over a decade.

  1997: Deep Blue defeats Kasparov #Milestone
  IBM's chess computer beats world champion 3.5 – 2.5.
  First major public AI milestone — sparks global media attention.

  2002: Torch ML framework (Collobert, Ronan — IDIAP)
  First major open-source neural network library.

  2003: PageRank & Google — ML in production at scale #Google
  Demonstrates large-scale statistical learning on real-world data.

  2005: DARPA Grand Challenge — Stanley wins (Stanford, Thrun) #Milestone
  First autonomous vehicle completes 132-mile desert route.
  Proves machine perception and planning can work in the real world.

## Deep Learning Renaissance (2006–2011) #era

  2006: Deep Belief Networks (Geoffrey Hinton) #Hinton #Milestone
  "A Fast Learning Algorithm for Deep Belief Nets" — Nature paper.
  Shows how to train deep networks layer by layer (unsupervised pre-training).
  Term "Deep Learning" enters mainstream AI vocabulary.

  2007: NVIDIA CUDA — GPU computing for science #Milestone
  General-purpose GPU programming unlocks massive parallelism.
  Training time for neural networks drops from weeks to hours.

  2010: Restricted Boltzmann Machines + Deep Boltzmann Machines (Hinton, Salakhutdinov)
  Generative deep models enabling unsupervised feature learning.

  2011: Apple Siri — AI enters consumer devices #Milestone
  First mainstream AI assistant. Wakes the world up to conversational AI.

  2011: IBM Watson wins Jeopardy! #Milestone
  Hybrid symbolic + statistical AI defeats champions Jennings and Rutter.
  Demonstrates NLP at human expert level on open-domain questions.

## Modern Deep Learning (2012–2016) #era

  2012: AlexNet wins ImageNet — the moment everything changed #Hinton #Milestone
  Krizhevsky, Sutskever & Hinton: 84.7% top-5 accuracy, crushing the 2nd-place 73.8%.
  Ignites the deep learning revolution. Every major tech company pivots to deep learning.

  2013: Word2Vec (Mikolov et al., Google) #Google #Milestone
  Word embeddings: dense vector representations capture semantic relationships.
  "King - Man + Woman ≈ Queen" — meaning is geometry.

  2014: DeepMind acquired by Google ($500M) #DeepMind #Google
  Founded in 2010 by Demis Hassabis, Shane Legg, Mustafa Suleyman.
  Deep Q-Networks (DQN) play Atari games from raw pixels.

  2015: OpenAI founded #OpenAI #Milestone
  Non-profit AI safety lab founded by Sam Altman, Elon Musk, Ilya Sutskever, Greg Brockman.
  Mission: ensure AI benefits all of humanity.
  2016: AlphaGo defeats Lee Sedol (4–1) #DeepMind #Milestone
  Defeats 9-dan world Go champion — a game with more positions than atoms in the universe.
  Combines deep RL + Monte Carlo tree search + supervised learning.
  Considered AI's "Sputnik moment."

  2016: Keras — high-level deep learning API (François Chollet)
  Makes deep learning accessible to a generation of practitioners.

## Transformer Era (2017–present) #era

  2017: "Attention Is All You Need" — the Transformer (Vaswani et al., Google Brain) #Google #Milestone
  Self-attention replaces recurrence entirely for sequence modeling.
  Enables massive parallelization during training.
  Every modern LLM is a descendant of this architecture.

  2018: GPT-1 (OpenAI) #OpenAI
  Generative Pre-trained Transformer: 117M parameters, unidirectional.
  Demonstrates that large-scale unsupervised pre-training transfers well.

  2019: GPT-2 (OpenAI — 1.5B parameters) #OpenAI #Milestone
  Generates coherent multi-paragraph text.
  OpenAI stages release, citing "misuse concerns" — first major AI safety debate.

  2020: GPT-3 (OpenAI — 175B parameters) #OpenAI #Milestone
  In-context learning: performs tasks with just a few examples in the prompt.
  Demonstrates emergent capabilities at scale — few-shot, zero-shot reasoning.

  2021: GitHub Copilot (OpenAI Codex) #OpenAI
  AI pair programmer trained on billions of lines of code.
  First AI tool to transform software development workflows.

  2021: AlphaFold releases protein structure database — 200M+ proteins #DeepMind
  Free database covering virtually every known protein.

  2022-11-30: ChatGPT launched (OpenAI) #OpenAI #Milestone
  InstructGPT + RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback).
  Reaches 1 million users in 5 days. 100 million in 2 months.
  Fastest-growing consumer application in history.

  2023-02: LLaMA (Meta AI) — open-source LLM #Meta #Milestone
  7B to 65B parameter open weights model.
  Enables open-source LLM ecosystem: Alpaca, Vicuna, Mistral...

  2025: Reasoning + Multi-modality converge
  GPT-4o, Claude 3.7 Sonnet, Gemini 2.5 — all natively multi-modal + reasoning.
  Open-source catches up: DeepSeek R1, LLaMA 3.3, Mistral Large.
